<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Northern Satraps</h1><p class="st-meta">20 BCE – 30 CE (approx.) · capital Mathura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Saka governors who ruled Mathura around the turn of the era and left the famous Mathura Lion Capital.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">Satraps</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a> governors who took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>, probably in the 1st century BCE, as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saka">Saka</a> power spread from the north-west. Their best-known ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajuvula">Rajuvula</a>, styled himself great satrap and issued coins copying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> designs.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> ruled a city that was already a major religious centre. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura-lion-capital">Mathura Lion Capital</a>, inscribed in Kharoshthi, records gifts to the Buddhist community by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajuvula">Rajuvula's</a> chief queen. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sodasa">Sodasa</a> issued inscriptions in Brahmi, including one recording a temple to Vasudeva, which shows the rulers supporting both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jain</a> communities also flourished in the city.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Within a few decades the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a> absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>, which became its southern capital and a major centre of sculpture. The satrapal system survived further south among the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 20 BCE – 30 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:59.30%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>20 BCE – 30 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mathura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rajuvula, Sodasa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Vaishnavism, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit, Gandhari Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Satraps of Mathura, Mahakshatrapas of Mathura</dd></dl></aside></div>

